+Also called: Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO) Traversal, Hierarchical Grid Traversal
 
 ## Overview
-Octree and Tree Traversal methods determine the sequence of nodes in a sparse voxel octree (SVO) or adaptive octree intersected by a ray, enabling efficient skipping of empty space and early termination for visibility testing [S1, S6, unofficial]. Unlike uniform grid ray marching which samples every voxel, these algorithms use axis-aligned slab intersection logic and child selection based on ray position relative to node centers to descend only into occupied subtrees [S1, S6, unofficial]. They differ from BVH-based approaches by operating directly on the octree structure without an intermediate bounding volume hierarchy, and from rasterization by rendering the entire scene with a single draw call and no vertex buffers [S6, unofficial].
+Octree and Tree Traversal methods determine which nodes of a hierarchical spatial structure (typically an octree or sparse voxel octree) a ray intersects, enabling efficient rendering of sparse volumetric data by skipping empty space [S1][S6, unofficial]. Unlike uniform grid ray marching which samples every voxel, these methods use the tree hierarchy to jump over empty regions, reducing computational cost [S1][S6, unofficial]. They differ from BVH-based approaches by operating directly on the octree structure without an intermediate bounding volume hierarchy, although some implementations may convert the SVO to a BVH for specific traversal optimizations [S1][S6, unofficial].
 
